            <div xml:id="d661">

                <pb facs="0647" n="635" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg635"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/892: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-06-18T17:00:00+09:00">June 18, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 18—7:23 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>162. Embassy’s 161, June 18, 4 p.m. In handing to me today the Japanese <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> concerning naval gun calibres, the
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs said that this was not a matter concerning only the
                    United States and Japan but that it concerned the world. He therefore wished to
                    observe that Japan’s inability to return a favorable answer was no indication of
                    any lack of friendliness on the part of Japan for the United States. He hopes
                    that as time goes by and the general international atmosphere is improved some
                    favorable solution of the naval problem will come “from somewhere”.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
